WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.750
- [Narrator] Humans need
dentists. It's a fact.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.558
In 2017 alone,

00:00:05.558 --> 00:00:10.549
Americans spent $129
billion on dental visits,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.740
and an estimated 90% of adults
in the US have fillings.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.870
But many animals don't have this problem

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.390
because they can regrow their teeth,

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.390
replacing old damaged ones
with brand new pearly whites.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.860
Wouldn't it be great if you could do that?

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.690
Well, there's actually
an important trade-off

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.043
for this nuisance.

00:00:35.155 --> 00:00:39.400
Only a handful of mammals can
regrow teeth multiple times

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:43.900
compared to the 50,000
species of reptiles and fish.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.220
Take geckos for example.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:51.220
They replace all 100 or so teeth
every three to four months,

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.940
and since geckos can
live for six to 10 years,

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.250
they'll grow anywhere from 1,800

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.330
to 4,000 teeth in a lifetime.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.740
That's all thanks to a special
type of cell in their gums

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.680
called stem cells.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.730
Stem cells are handy because
they can morph into different

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.170
cells when needed,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.133
like tooth stem cells to build new teeth.

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:15.880
Humans have these stem
cells when we're younger,

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.090
but after our adult teeth grow in,

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.930
the stem cells die and disappear.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.920
To understand why, let's
take a journey back in time

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.490
to about 320 million
years ago when mammals

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.800
and reptiles split off.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.270
In addition to many obvious differences,

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.260
another change that eventually
emerged was tooth shape.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.660
Reptiles, for example, are
what's called generalists,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:44.220
meaning they eat the animals
they can get their teeth on.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.640
And for that, they needed teeth
with the same size and shape

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.760
to keep prey from escaping their mouths.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.700
Mammals, on the other hand,
developed more specific diets,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.040
like grazers who only eat
grass and hunters who rip flesh

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.610
from their kills.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.830
As a result, mammals evolved
different-shaped teeth

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.510
for different purposes.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.760
And it's this difference
that could also explain

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.590
why mammals can't regrow more teeth.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:14.200
Now, let's say you could regrow
your molars multiple times,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.400
for example.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.650
It's important that the top
and bottom sets match up.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:22.080
Otherwise, they can't
grind food as efficiently.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.060
That sounds good in principle,
but with each new set,

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.760
there's a risk that the
regrown teeth won't line up.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.802
So the leading theory is that
adult humans can't regrow

00:02:32.802 --> 00:02:34.410
our teeth because it
was better for survival

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.310
to only grow one well-aligned adult set.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.520
However, if you still wish
you could regrow a tooth,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.820
there may be a way in the future.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.970
Using lasers and drugs, scientists
have helped rats and mice

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.229
regrow damaged tissue
in cavity-ridden teeth

00:02:52.229 --> 00:02:55.840
with the idea that if you
could regrow tooth tissue,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.290
you could eventually regrow entire teeth,

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.580
though no human testing
has been done on that yet.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.570
So keep flossing and
keep seeing your dentist.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:07.593
At least for now.

